@page "/subscriptions"

<PageTitle>Subscriptions - Linq2GraphQL</PageTitle>

<h1 class="page-heading">Subscriptions</h1>
<p class="lead text-secondary">
    A subscription keeps a connection open and delivers an event each time something happens on the server.
    Linq2GraphQL exposes it as an <code>IObservable</code>, shaped with <code>Select</code> like any query, over
    WebSockets or server-sent events.
</p>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="setup">Setup</h2>
    <p>Subscriptions are opt-in in the generator and live in their own package:</p>
    <CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="@Setup"/>
    <p>
        Without <code>-s</code> the generated client has no <code>Subscription</code> property, and the generator warns
        if the schema has subscriptions.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="subscribe">Subscribe</h2>
    <p>
        The examples on this page use the repository's test server, which publishes <code>customerAdded</code> each
        time the <code>addCustomer</code> mutation runs. <code>StartAsync</code> returns the observable, and you
        subscribe to it:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Subscribe"/>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Every subscriber gets its own connection, opened when it subscribes and closed when the subscription
            returned by <code>Subscribe</code> is disposed.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>Select</code> works as for queries: <code>Select()</code> takes every scalar field, a projection
            requests only the fields it reads. See <a href="selecting-fields">selecting fields</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
            When the server completes the stream, the observable completes. A transport failure or an error event
            ends it with <code>OnError</code>.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="protocols">Transports</h2>
    <p>Pick the transport with <code>SubscriptionProtocol</code> in the client options:</p>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table table-vcenter card-table border">
            <thead>
            <tr><th><code>SubscriptionProtocol</code></th><th>Transport</th><th>Address</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>GraphQLWebSocket</code> (default)</td>
                <td>WebSockets with the <a href="https://github.com/enisdenjo/graphql-ws" target="_blank" rel="noopener">graphql-ws</a> protocol (subprotocol <code>graphql-transport-ws</code>).</td>
                <td rowspan="2">The client's base address with <code>ws://</code> or <code>wss://</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>ApolloWebSocket</code></td>
                <td>The older Apollo protocol (subprotocol <code>graphql-ws</code>), for servers that don't speak graphql-ws yet.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>ServerSentEvents</code></td>
                <td>Server-sent events over a plain HTTP request.</td>
                <td>The client's base address.</td>
            </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Protocol"/>
    <p>
        Server-sent events go through the same HTTP pipeline as queries, so <code>ConfigureRequest</code>,
        <code>WithAccessToken</code> and your <code>HttpClient</code> handlers apply to them. The same hooks also
        run for the WebSocket transports: the headers they produce are copied onto every upgrade request
        (initial connect and each reconnect), so a refreshed token is picked up automatically.
    </p>
    <p>
        Blazor WebAssembly is the exception: the browser does not allow custom headers on WebSocket requests.
        Set <code>SendAuthorizationInConnectionInit = true</code> to send the hook's
        <code>Authorization</code> header inside the <code>connection_init</code> payload instead — the
        graphql-ws/Apollo convention of an <code>Authorization</code> connection parameter. For any other
        connection parameters, use <code>ConnectionInitPayload</code>:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@ConnectionInit"/>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="errors">Errors in events</h2>
    <p>
        With <code>StartAsync</code>, an event that carries GraphQL errors ends the subscription with
        <code>OnError</code> and a <code>GraphQueryExecutionException</code>. When the server can send partial data,
        use <code>StartWithErrorsAsync</code> instead. Each event then arrives as a <code>GraphResult</code> with its
        data and errors, and an event with errors doesn't end the stream:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@WithErrors"/>
    <p>Transport and protocol failures end the stream in both cases.</p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="reliability">Connections that drop, stall or fall behind</h2>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table table-vcenter card-table border">
            <thead>
            <tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>SubscriptionRetryAttempts</code></td>
                <td><code>5</code></td>
                <td>
                    WebSockets: how often a dropped connection is reopened before the subscription fails with an
                    <code>IOException</code>. Attempts back off exponentially with jitter, like the graphql-ws
                    reference client, and a successful reconnect resets the count. Close codes that mean the server
                    won't accept the connection are never retried. <code>0</code> fails on the first drop.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>SubscriptionIdleTimeout</code></td>
                <td>none</td>
                <td>
                    Server-sent events: fail the subscription when nothing, not even a keep-alive comment, arrives within
                    this time. A WebSocket connection that receives nothing for 30 seconds is always treated as lost
                    and reconnected.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>SubscriptionMaxBufferedEvents</code></td>
                <td><code>128</code></td>
                <td>How many events are held for a subscriber that is slower than the server.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>SubscriptionOverflowPolicy</code></td>
                <td><code>Fault</code></td>
                <td>
                    What happens when that buffer is full. <code>Fault</code> fails the subscription, so events are
                    never lost silently. <code>DropOldest</code> drops the oldest queued events, and
                    <code>KeepLatest</code> keeps only the newest, for subscribers that only care about the current
                    value.
                </td>
            </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Reliability"/>
</section>

@code {
    private const string Setup = """
        Linq2GraphQL https://example.com/graphql -s -c=SampleClient -n=Sample -o=Generated
        dotnet add package Linq2GraphQL.Client.Subscriptions --prerelease
        """;

    private const string Subscribe = """
        var customers = await sampleClient
            .Subscription
            .CustomerAdded()
            .Select(c => new { c.CustomerId, c.CustomerName })
            .StartAsync();

        using var subscription = customers.Subscribe(
            customer => Console.WriteLine($"Added: {customer.CustomerName}"),
            error => Console.WriteLine($"Subscription failed: {error.Message}"),
            () => Console.WriteLine("The server ended the subscription"));

        // ... events arrive until the subscription is disposed
        """;

    private const string Protocol = """
        builder.Services
            .AddSampleClient(options =>
            {
                options.SubscriptionProtocol = SubscriptionProtocol.ServerSentEvents;
            })
            .WithHttpClient(httpClient => httpClient.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://example.com/graphql"));
        """;

    private const string ConnectionInit = """
        builder.Services
            .AddSampleClient(options =>
            {
                // Blazor WebAssembly: headers are not allowed on WebSocket requests,
                // so the token travels in the connection_init payload.
                options.SendAuthorizationInConnectionInit = true;

                // Any other connection parameters your server expects:
                options.ConnectionInitPayload = (client, cancellationToken) => ValueTask.FromResult<object>(
                    new Dictionary<string, object>
                    {
                        ["authToken"] = tokenService.CurrentToken,
                        ["clientVersion"] = "1.4",
                    });
            });
        """;

    private const string WithErrors = """
        var events = await sampleClient
            .Subscription
            .CustomerAdded()
            .Select()
            .StartWithErrorsAsync();

        using var subscription = events.Subscribe(result =>
        {
            if (result.HasErrors)
            {
                logger.LogWarning("Event with errors: {Message}", result.Errors[0].Message);
            }

            if (result.HasData)
            {
                Handle(result.Data);
            }
        });
        """;

    private const string Reliability = """
        builder.Services
            .AddSampleClient(options =>
            {
                options.SubscriptionRetryAttempts = 10;
                options.SubscriptionMaxBufferedEvents = 1000;
                options.SubscriptionOverflowPolicy = SubscriptionOverflowPolicy.DropOldest;
            })
            .WithHttpClient(httpClient => httpClient.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://example.com/graphql"));
        """;
}
